#{[#edb/ref 17592186048146 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808} :activity/id #uuid "37dbb8fe-57e2-5578-a0d2-de00af96f5ed" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & ={{(\\,7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|}}-{{(\\,1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|}}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{13}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $[\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{aligned}]$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{aligned}| & =a|\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{aligned}|-b|\\begin{aligned}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{aligned}|+c|\\begin{aligned}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059452 :question/id #uuid "14e05d4f-373c-5d1f-aa59-7ee52466480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14762" :question/problem "Find the value of ![](images/8e/8e7935fce161e2a1ed706ec03bb5cd202e8c4650ba01ca5e4633f9f1c121d04a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0(8+96)+3(8+48)+6(-96+48) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 56+6\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =168-288 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061007 :tutorial/content "If the determinant of a square matrix $A$ is equal to zero, then we say that $A$ is a **singular** matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is singular, then it does not have a multiplicative inverse." :tutorial/title "Singular Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059453 :question/id #uuid "d7516cec-bd96-59db-98cd-5d82e41fa719" :question/math-academy-id "e-14763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n![](images/21/21cd71e00c682ad1117b9ae126adcbeb8e4c55769c73eae1e1169534f2a01d4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061008 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthat has an unknown constant $k$ as one of the entries, and we are given that $A$ is singular. How do we find all the possible values of $k?$\n\nOne way is to use the formula for the determinant of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k,$ and then make an equation with the given determinant to solve for $k.$\n\nSo first, let's use the formula for the determinant on our matrix to get an expression in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1-2)-2(k-0)+3(2k-0) \\\\  & =-3-2k+6k \\\\  & =4k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we are told that $\\det(A) =0,$ let's equate the above expression to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ 4k-3 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $k$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" :tutorial/title "Solving for a Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059454 :question/id #uuid "7780340c-43d6-5d38-9270-865914098218" :question/math-academy-id "e-14764" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of  $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is $2,$ find the value of the constant $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(12-10)-1(3k-0)+2(2k-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3k+2\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =6-3k+4k \\\\  & =k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}}] :activity/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059886}] [#edb/ref 17592186048233 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116367} :activity/id #uuid "b5ef0f4d-28ef-57bb-a6d8-a44c7baf171f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116440 :tutorial/content "Many of the known properties of derivatives of scalar functions extend naturally to derivatives of vector functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions, $u(t)$ is a scalar function, and $\\alpha$ and $\\mathbf c$ are scalar and vector constants, respectively. \n\nThe **sum and multiple rules** for differentiating vector-valued functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)+{g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{sum rule} \\\\ (\\alpha f)^{'}(t)=\\alpha {f}^{'}(t),\\,(uc)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)c\\, & \\text{constant multiple rules} \\\\ (uf)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{scalar function multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116441 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiating vector-valued functions states that\n\n![](images/57/57f880045af93f552c2736659e377c7ac4c6f41f78a2df446f241783f472f8aa.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcf7f1c428a975daf939f469deb8e13c050d912fb5a2548fbb6b60ea6d413a86.svg) is a vector-valued function while ![](images/cd/cd94c3404a0b6460dd11ffac737b2ce09af9f26b21de28ccedf8d028bd6ec38a.svg) is a scalar function." :tutorial/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116442 :tutorial/content "When working with vector functions, there are two types of products: the dot product and the cross product. So, there are two **product rules** for derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{'}(t)=f(t)\\cdot {g}^{'}(t)+{f}^{'}(t)\\cdot g(t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ (f\\times g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Remember that the cross product is anticommutative, meaning that $\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g = - \\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g)(t) = -\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f)(t).\n$$\n\nWhen applying the cross product rule, the order in which the operations are applied is important." :tutorial/title "The Product Rules for Differentiation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114970 :question/id #uuid "ce098ff9-2ef1-5483-b057-b3fef12af16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2477" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t,$ where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i-(\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(4t-1)\\,i+{e}^{3t}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{t-2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k, \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)k \\\\  & =4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+2g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+2{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\,i+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)+2(4\\,i+3{e}^{3t}\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\,j-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114971 :question/id #uuid "3668609c-7f98-53ef-8aa7-6f9c3209dfee" :question/math-academy-id "e-2478" :question/problem "Find $\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where ![](images/9e/9e23d542bfc56d506f5437a35a92c304d618109ef60086e1f5a7a4fee57d8a80.png) and $u(t) = t.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)g(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)g(t)+u(t){g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+t\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114972 :question/id #uuid "cbb15346-028c-59c0-9b20-cd2f408c3bcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2479" :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t),$ if $\\mathbf f(u) = \\ln u \\, \\mathbf i + (u^2+5) \\, \\mathbf j + 4u \\, \\mathbf k$ and $u(t) =2t^3.$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\,i+2u\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\,i+2u(t)\\,j+4\\,k){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\,i+2(2{t}^{3})\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+24{t}^{5}\\,j+24{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114973 :question/id #uuid "228ae66f-9dad-5e6d-b405-9fbe62ecb7e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2480" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t),$ given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{t}^{2}j \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{1}{t}i+{e}^{t}j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f} \\cdot \\mathbf{g})(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t))\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}f(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+2t\\,j, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}g(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t)\n&= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t)) \\[3pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\,\\mathbf i +2t \\, \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + e^t \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) +\n\\left(\\sqrt{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + t^2 \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{t^2}  \\, \\mathbf i + e^t \\, \\mathbf j  \\right) \\[3pt]\t\t \n&=\\dfrac{1}{2t\\sqrt{t}}  +2te^t    +  \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2}\\right)    + t^2e^t \\[3pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2}  -  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2} +2te^t   + t^2e^t  \\[3pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2} +\\left(2t+t^2\\right)e^t.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}}] :activity/title "Differentiation Rules for Vector-Valued Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115483}] [#edb/ref 17592186048219 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064} :activity/id #uuid "24e4f3d3-57f6-51a9-afd6-0c3e9a2f8f97" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130392 :tutorial/content "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nRecall that, under this notation,\n\n- $\\mathbf u = [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n- $\\mathbf x = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that\n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129006 :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ce/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/59/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/40/40212b3cc1bb25e4d42b96ba9fd2c1958772a32fe69136dca347be295956b9ea.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/de/de70592e2668d2ef6030843fe9e20dd3da9fc1edec2501e7cd318a57682f3f0e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/9b/9b0ee6271962e3ede406351f05d805ae60f4b37ce5a45489b117ab3786ed3c23.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/72/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$\n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = \\mathbf{T}\\left( [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}v & -u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ & =u{cos}^{2}v+u{sin}^{2}v \\\\ & =u({cos}^{2}v+{sin}^{2}v) \\\\ & =u.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130461 :tutorial/content "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation\n\n$$\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." :tutorial/title "A Review of Affine Transformations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130462 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones.\n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$J(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson).\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$.\n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)< 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128860 :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](images/4c/4c41597b6c647ba16bff7b6675d1bbe9d3c66b8cbb8e9a13bbb6651da6cd284e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128861 :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418" :question/problem "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/4c/4cb994a50c42c48bad095fb056406cdcebd42143b3c0ce041ad06f52a2942a01.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](images/e9/e947cb962ec1c0405292be18c838205b7be95ab083e07925d797b87e9c5b8654.png)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/2b/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129344}] [#edb/ref 17592186048185 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199281} :activity/id #uuid "3436fa6e-7d06-5a5e-90bb-b6107d2ecf0e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198020 :question/id #uuid "9a1112c0-db94-5572-ba53-b78116b7c273" :question/math-academy-id "e-12355" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) =\\left\\langle x+y,\\: x-y,\\: xyz \\right\\rangle, \\quad \\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u^2,\\: v^2 ,\\: 1 \\right\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d7/d7b8d89cae048a9cd5e04299bebdcb3a5d5d21ac6d5a31c48482aa00ca71517c.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\,x-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,4uv⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199465 :tutorial/content "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198022 :question/id #uuid "0364a11f-e567-517e-acf2-471708dbdc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12358" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\left\\langle z, \\: x, \\: y \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle 1+2u+v, \\: u+v, \\: u-v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩|}_{x=1+2u+v\\,y=u+v\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\,1+2u+v,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u-v,\\,1+2u+v,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (-2)+(1+2u+v)\\cdot 3+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+2v+3+6u+3v+u+v \\\\  & =5u+6v+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u+6v+3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+6v+3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[5uv+3{v}^{2}+3v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5u+6)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2}+6u]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Parametric Surfaces" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198487}]}
